  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nWhen libpsl is enabled and a response host is itself a public suffix (e.g. `github.io`), a `Set-Cookie` header whose `Domain` attribute exactly equals that host is accepted by `psl_is_cookie_domain_acceptable()` but stored with `tailmatch = TRUE`. RFC 6265 §5.3 requires this exact-match case to produce a host-only cookie. Because curl keeps it domain-scoped, the cookie is sent to any sibling host (e.g. `attacker.github.io`) on a subsequent request or redirect.\n\n## Affected versions\n\nThe vulnerable code path exists in at least curl 8.21.0 (latest GA release) and the current 8.22.0-DEV tree. The `parse_domain()` logic has been unchanged across many prior releases. libpsl must be enabled at build time (`--with-libpsl`), which most distribution packages do.\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `lib/cookie.c`, `parse_domain()` unconditionally sets `co->tailmatch = TRUE` for every explicit non-IP `Domain` attribute (line ~512):\n\n```c\nif(!is_ip)\n  co->tailmatch = TRUE;\n```\n\nLater, `is_public_suffix()` calls `psl_is_cookie_domain_acceptable(psl, \"github.io\", \"github.io\")`. libpsl intentionally returns \"acceptable\" for exact equality — the caller is expected to handle the host-only conversion. curl never does.\n\nWhen `Curl_cookie_getlist()` builds the outgoing `Cookie` header, it calls `cookie_tailmatch(\"github.io\", 9, \"attacker.github.io\")` which returns `TRUE` (suffix match with a preceding `.`), so the cookie leaks.\n\n## Reproduction\n1. Build curl at commit c2676bf9e639e31e44b12a2cc82cf2046231b056 (or any version from 8.21.0 onward) on a 64-bit Linux or macOS host, ensuring libpsl is enabled (--with-libpsl or -DCURL_USE_LIBPSL=ON). Confirm the build includes libpsl by checking that curl --version output contains libpsl.\n\n2. Start a local HTTP server on port 8080 that serves two endpoints:\n\n/set-domain — responds with 302, sets Set-Cookie: sid=SECRET; Domain=github.io; Path=/, and redirects to http://attacker.github.io:8080/sink.\n/sink — responds with 200 and echoes back the received Cookie header value in the response body.\n\n3. Map the test hostnames to localhost using curl's --resolve flag:\n\ngithub.io:8080:127.0.0.1\nattacker.github.io:8080:127.0.0.1\n\n4. Run the vulnerable request — make a request to http://github.io:8080/set-domain with redirect following enabled (-L) and a cookie jar (-c /tmp/domain.jar):\n\n```\ncurl -sS -L --noproxy '*' \\\n  --resolve github.io:8080:127.0.0.1 \\\n  --resolve attacker.github.io:8080:127.0.0.1 \\\n  -c /tmp/domain.jar \\\n  http://github.io:8080/set-domain\n```\n\n5. Observe the response body — it will contain sid=SECRET, proving the cookie was sent to attacker.github.io (the redirect target), a sibling host under the same public suffix.\n\n6. Inspect the cookie jar — the entry for the domain cookie will show .github.io with the subdomain-match flag set to TRUE, confirming it was incorrectly stored as domain-scoped.\n\n7. Run a negative control — repeat the same flow but have the server set the cookie without an explicit Domain attribute (i.e., Set-Cookie: sid=HOST_ONLY; Path=/). The redirect target at attacker.github.io will receive <none> — the cookie is correctly not sent. The cookie jar will show github.io with subdomain-match FALSE (host-only).\n\n8. Run a second negative control — issue the same Domain=github.io cookie from a sibling host (tenant.github.io) instead of the apex. Curl correctly rejects it — the cookie jar will be empty and nothing is sent to the redirect target.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAfter `psl_is_cookie_domain_acceptable()` accepts exact equality between the request host and a public-suffix cookie domain, clear `tailmatch` so the cookie becomes host-only. Non-exact public-suffix Domain attempts should continue to be rejected. Concretely, after the PSL acceptance in `Curl_cookie_add()`, add:\n\n```c\nif(co->tailmatch && domain && co->domain &&\n   curl_strequal(co->domain, domain) &&\n   psl_is_public_suffix(psl, lcookie))\n  co->tailmatch = FALSE;\n```\n\n## Impact\n\n## Summary:\nAn attacker controlling a sibling host under a public suffix (trivial on hosting platforms like GitHub Pages, Heroku, Netlify) can receive cookies that the public-suffix apex issued with an explicit `Domain` matching itself. The attacker cannot *plant* the cookie — exploitation requires the apex to issue such a cookie and the curl client to subsequently contact the attacker sibling. The PSL boundary that libpsl is intended to enforce is violated.",
